A||||9||mbramble||Broken EGR Tube||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||12-08-2001||02:36 PM||||What kind of problems does this cause??  I just found it was broken -- completely about 2" from the EGR valve.  <P>Is it repairable??  I wrapped it in steel wire to hold the two ends together and was thinking of using some of that muffler repair stuff on it to seal the 'connection'.<P>It was covered in heavy insulation.  Is the insulation required?<P>Any information would be most appreciated.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||03:53 PM||||it can cause an idle of 3,000 rpm at start up, flawed EGR performance - and every problem inbetween. One solution is to take it off and see about silver soldering it, or ( which I would do ) forget about fixing it and buy the replacement unit from the Fiero Store and never worry about it again, also I would check with Rodney Dickman to see if he makes a replacement unit  - his merchandise, attitude, and service are far superior to the Fiero Store.<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000002||REDHOTT88||12-08-2001||04:36 PM||||West Coast Fiero: <P>That was VERY nice to say about a fellow Fiero parts competitor. <P>Keep up the good attitude!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||12-08-2001||05:05 PM||||just comes from experience with the Fiero Store. I am grateful that they have so many parts, but from experience, and that of other customers - they are only in the Fiero game for the buck, where as Rodney, Archie, and several others who are just now getting started ( and those who I don't yet know of ) are out to contribute greatly to the Fiero community<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||12-08-2001||05:18 PM||||A new one is about $80 from a GM dealer, and they are still available.  The 2.8 MPFI F-bodies have a similar tube, but the flanges won't match on one end (thanks for the thought, GM).  <P>I was able to use some ultra-high temp sealant from Pep Boys to fix mine, called Inferno Metal Repair, and so far it's been holding since August.  I used the sealant, followed by a small stainless steel hose clamp for strength, then another layer of the sealant.  The stuff is like cement, and it is supposedly good to 2000 degrees.  Under $10 to fix, and I started with a 1/2" crack inside one of the ripples of the tube.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||12-08-2001||06:09 PM||||The next time I replace one, itll be the stainless braided one, never have to do it again. $90. Last time I replaced used S10, 2.8 for direct bolt in.||||reg||1
Z||000006||tesmith66||12-09-2001||12:40 PM||||I got one for $1.00 at the boneyard from a 2.8 liter Firebird. I just elongated the mounting holes on one end with a Dremel and put it on with new gaskets. It's been on there for over a year with no problems.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||14
